  "response": "The supplied evidence does not show a single test that explicitly combines a cross-host redirect, stripping an `Authorization` header, and then replacing it with netrc credentials.\n\nThe netrc credential behavior is verified by `test_basicauth_with_netrc` in `tests/test_requests.py` (the supplied excerpt is at `tests/test_requests.py:L640-L786`). It temporarily replaces `requests.sessions.get_netrc_auth`, confirms that the returned netrc credentials succeed, and confirms that explicitly supplied wrong credentials override netrc credentials and receive HTTP 401. [Data: Sources (85); Entities (1151, 1160, 1159)]\n\nThe header-stripping behavior is covered separately by `test_should_strip_auth_host_change`, which expects stripping when the host changes, and `test_auth_is_retrained_for_redirect_on_host` is not present; the supplied entity is actually `test_auth_is_retained_for_redirect_on_host`, which verifies that same-host redirects retain `Authorization`. `test_auth_is_stripped_on_http_downgrade` verifies removal on an HTTPS-to-HTTP redirect. [Data: Entities (1290, 1289, 1288)]\n\nThe implementation path is described by `rebuild_auth`: it removes `Authorization` when `should_strip_auth(...)` is true, then obtains netrc credentials and applies them when trusted environment settings permit. Thus, the snapshot supports the implementation condition and the related tests, but is insufficient to identify a test proving the complete cross-host strip-and-netrc-replacement sequence. [Data: Entities (789, 762)]",
  "score": 86,
  "follow_up_queries": [
    "Which source test explicitly follows a cross-host redirect and checks whether netrc credentials are reapplied?",
    "What exact `trust_env` and request-auth conditions control netrc replacement inside `rebuild_auth`?"
  ]
